WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.780
Welcome back. In this video, we'll look at
who owns and who

2
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.920
manages data centers. As you'll see, the
answer depends on the

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.460
business model, the scale, and the purpose
of the data center.

4
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:18.660
Let's start with ownership. As of 2024,
about

5
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.620
60% of the global IT workload is hosted in
hyperscale

6
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.600
data centers, those owned or leased

7
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.040
by companies like Amazon, Google,
Microsoft.

8
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.240
These providers either build their own
facilities or lease space

9
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.120
in massive campuses. Hyperscale facilities
are

10
00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.900
large, efficient, and designed for scale.
When

11
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.700
hyperscale companies own the data center,
they're responsible for

12
00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.720
everything, from construction to energy
efficiency to managing

13
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.420
IT operations. When they lease, they

14
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.440
manage the IT equipment and typically have
stringent requirements for

15
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.020
complete floors in the data center. But

16
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.940
ultimately, they rely on a colocation
partner to manage the

17
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:09.300
physical facility. Now, let's talk about

18
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.100
non-hyperscale environments. These include

19
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:17.240
enterprise-owned data centers and
colocation facilities.

20
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.760
Together, they account for about 40% of
the market.

21
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.640
An enterprise-owned data center is built
and operated by a

22
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:31.440
single organization, usually to support
internal IT needs.

23
00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.000
Think of a bank, a government agency, or
an

24
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.640
enterprise running its own data center for
compliance and control.

25
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.540
In a colocation model, multiple companies
rent space,

26
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.260
power, and cooling from a specialized
third-party

27
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:53.600
data center provider, but they install and
manage their

28
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.280
own servers and IT equipment. This

29
00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.340
model offers flexibility and reduces the
need for capital investment.

30
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.800
So who manages what? Let's break it down.

31
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:10.639
Management in a data center is divided
into two core functions:

32
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.880
IT infrastructure management and facility
infrastructure

33
00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.860
management. Building management is about
keeping the lights on,

34
00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:22.640
literally. This includes power
distribution,

35
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.600
HVAC systems, physical security, and
cooling.

36
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.840
It's often managed through a BMS, or
building management

37
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.660
system. IT management, on the other

38
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.620
hand, focuses on the servers, storage, and
networking

39
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.500
systems. These are tracked and managed

40
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.160
using a DCIM, or a data center
infrastructure

41
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:51.260
management system. DCIM tools provide for
visibility

42
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.080
into how IT assets are performing and
consuming resources.

43
00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:00.060
These two separate systems, BMS and DCIM,
work

44
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.720
together. One manages the environment, the
other

45
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.660
manages the workload. When integrated,
they allow

46
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.660
operators to respond proactively to issues
and optimize performance.

47
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.020
But data center management goes beyond
just facilities

48
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.160
and IT. A modern data center also operates
as a

49
00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.940
business unit with responsibilities that
include customer onboarding,

50
00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.000
contract management, sustainability, and
compliance with global

51
00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.580
standards. Some of these standards are ISO

52
00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.680
27000 for data security, ISO 14001 for

53
00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:40.680
environmental management, ISO 50001 for
energy

54
00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.600
use, and new regulations like the EU CSRD
directive.

55
00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.760
Together, these frameworks ensure that the
data center is efficient,

56
00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.760
secure, sustainable, and accountable.

57
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.580
Depending on the ownership model, either a
third-party provider,

58
00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:01.320
an internal enterprise team, or a cloud
provider manages

59
00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:05.160
these management layers. For example, in a
colocation

60
00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.120
model, the BMS is managed by the provider,
but the

61
00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.280
DCIM might be the tenant's responsibility.
In summary,

62
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.959
the key takeaway is this: ownership and
management are

63
00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.959
not always the same. You can own and
manage

64
00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.500
everything, lease and manage the IT only,

65
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.640
or outsource both. Understanding these

66
00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.320
roles is key to navigating today's complex
data center

67
00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:33.940
landscape.
